<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pali Canon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tipiṭaka, Tipitaka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pali Canon is the standard collection of scriptures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhist tradition, preserved in the Pāli language, and the most complete extant early Buddhist canon. According to Buddhist tradition its teachings were recited at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhist-councils">First Buddhist Council</a> after the Buddha's death and kept orally by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a>, then written down on palm leaves at the Fourth Buddhist Council in 29 BCE. It falls into three pitakas or baskets: the Vinaya Piṭaka on the discipline of the sangha, the Sutta Piṭaka of discourses and sermons, and the Abhidhamma Piṭaka of doctrinal treatises. The earliest fragments of canonical Pali come from the Pyu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, of the mid-5th to mid-6th century CE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The oldest complete collection to survive is the <b>Pali Canon</b> of the Theravada tradition, known as the Tipitaka or “three baskets”: the Vinaya, the rules of monastic life; the Sutta Pitaka, the discourses of the Buddha; and the Abhidhamma, systematic analyses of mind and experience.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li></ul></section>
